---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Privacy Policy — QuiiCast"
	description="What QuiiCast collects, what it doesn't, and how its peer-to-peer screen sharing actually handles data."
	path="/privacy-policy"
>
	<div class="text-center">
		<h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">Privacy Policy</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-xl text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			Written to actually reflect how QuiiCast works, not generic boilerplate.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="prose mx-auto mt-14 max-w-2xl">
		<h2>What QuiiCast collects</h2>
		<p>
			Starting a screen share creates a short-lived session record — the 4-digit code, the
			connection mode, and timestamps — stored just long enough for a receiver to look it up.
			That record is deleted automatically a few minutes after it's created; sessions are never
			kept longer than that.
		</p>
		<p>
			QuiiCast doesn't ask for a name, email address, or account of any kind to use it. The IP
			address of incoming connection requests is used briefly, in memory, to apply rate limiting
			against abuse — like repeatedly guessing codes — and is not logged to a database or
			retained afterward.
		</p>

		<h2>What QuiiCast doesn't collect</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>No accounts, no passwords, no email addresses</li>
			<li>No cookies, no analytics, no third-party trackers</li>
			<li>No recording — QuiiCast never captures, stores, or has access to the video or audio of a screen share</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>How the video actually works</h2>
		<p>
			Once two browsers are connected, video and audio travel directly between them over a
			peer-to-peer WebRTC connection. QuiiCast's server helps the two browsers find each other —
			looking up a code and briefly relaying connection details — but the screen-share content
			itself never passes through, and is never visible to, QuiiCast's server at any point.
		</p>

		<h2>Local storage</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast stores one small preference in your browser's local storage: whether you've chosen
			light or dark mode. It stays on your device and is never sent anywhere.
		</p>

		<h2>Third-party infrastructure</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast's signaling server and database run on standard cloud infrastructure providers. Those
			providers may keep their own operational logs as part of running their infrastructure,
			separate from anything QuiiCast itself collects or controls.
		</p>

		<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
		<p>If this policy changes, the update will be posted on this page.</p>

		<h2>Contact</h2>
		<p>
			Questions about this policy can be sent through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
